derivations.claim:
A height-critical degeneracy has equations $Y_x=Y_{xx}=0$, of codimension two on the two-dimensional source $(x,t)$. Transverse occurrence gives $Y_{xt}Y_{xxx}
e0$, since the derivative matrix of these equations has first entry $Y_{xx}=0$. The extra degeneracy $Y_{xxx}=0$ has codimension three and is absent by [F3]. Thus these are isolated ordinary births/deaths. Explicitly the implicit function theorem applied to $Y_x=0$ solves $t=T(x)$, with $T'(x_0)=0$ and $T''(x_0)=-Y_{xxx}/Y_{xt}
e0$. Taylor's integral formula gives $T(x)-t_0=(x-x_0)^2a(x)$ with smooth nonzero $a$; the smooth coordinate $u=(x-x_0)\sqrt{|a(x)|}$ makes the critical-point equation exactly $u^2\pm(t-t_0)=0$. At all other height-critical points $Y_{xx}
e0$, so they continue as smooth curves in time. A crossing preimage becomes critical when $g(x,t)=g(y,t)$ and $Y_x(x,t)=0$, with $x
e y$. These are three independent equations on $(x,y,t)$; their transverse occurrence means the derivative of $Y_x$ along the continued crossing branch is nonzero. Adding $Y_{xx}(x,t)=0$ is codimension four and is absent. The other crossing branch cannot also be height-critical, since that would give two parallel horizontal tangents at a regular crossing. Hence this event is exactly a crossing sliding through a nondegenerate maximum or minimum on one branch. [F2, F3, step 2.1, construct, algebra]

